WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.900
The first film raced microbes against the body's own defenses, but an antibiotic

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.640
attacks the microbes directly.

3
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:16.120
If it kills almost every bacterium, how can the infection come back?

4
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.660
To answer that, start with what the drug needs.

5
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:25.970
An antibiotic is a drug that kills bacteria or stops them growing and dividing.

6
00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.070
To work, it needs something to hit.

7
00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:34.190
Many antibiotics block the building of the bacterial cell wall.

8
00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.330
Others jam bacterial ribosomes, so the cell cannot make proteins.

9
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.800
Now look at a virus.

10
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.200
It has no cell wall and no ribosomes of its own.

11
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.300
It works inside one of your cells and uses that cell's ribosomes.

12
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.300
These drugs find no bacterial part there to hit.

13
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.040
So antibiotics do not treat colds or flu.

14
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:04.123
Back to bacteria, now a large population of them in an infection.

15
00:01:05.403 --> 00:01:08.963
Most are sensitive, which means the drug kills or stops them.

16
00:01:10.123 --> 00:01:13.923
But a few may already carry a gene that lets them survive the drug.

17
00:01:15.143 --> 00:01:16.143
These are resistant.

18
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:23.633
Antibiotic resistance is the ability of some bacteria to survive a drug that kills

19
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:24.853
others of their kind.

20
00:01:26.353 --> 00:01:29.853
Start the drug. Here it kills the sensitive cells.

21
00:01:31.373 --> 00:01:33.673
Round after round, the teal cells disappear.

22
00:01:34.973 --> 00:01:38.033
The orange ones survive, because they resist the drug.

23
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:40.913
Now every teal cell is gone.

24
00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:45.853
Only the few orange cells remain, with empty space around them.

25
00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:50.053
When this population grows back, what will it be made of?

26
00:01:56.433 --> 00:02:02.793
Resistant cells. With their competition gone, the orange cells divide and fill the

27
00:02:02.793 --> 00:02:05.853
space. The drug did not create them.

28
00:02:07.073 --> 00:02:09.533
In this example, they were there before the drug.

29
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:12.213
It removed their competition.

30
00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:18.973
This is natural selection: individuals with a helpful trait survive and reproduce

31
00:02:18.973 --> 00:02:23.873
more. Here the trait is resistance, and the drug did the selecting.

32
00:02:25.213 --> 00:02:27.293
The bacteria did not choose to change.

33
00:02:28.413 --> 00:02:30.693
And the person did not become resistant.

34
00:02:31.913 --> 00:02:33.113
The bacteria did.

35
00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:35.607
There is one more route.

36
00:02:36.767 --> 00:02:41.447
A resistant bacterium can pass a resistance gene to another bacterium.

37
00:02:42.527 --> 00:02:44.787
That neighbour now survives the drug too.

38
00:02:46.087 --> 00:02:49.687
So resistance can spread to bacteria that never met the drug.

39
00:02:50.717 --> 00:02:56.157
Killing almost every bacterium is not a cure if resistant survivors grow back.

40
00:02:57.497 --> 00:03:01.557
Using an antibiotic can favor any resistant bacteria present.

41
00:03:02.837 --> 00:03:07.277
An antibiotic spares resistant bacteria, and the survivors can take over.
